encyclopedia :reedwater_people do
  name "Reedwater People"
  summary "Reedwater People are Korvath channel families who mark adulthood with a first solo crossing through water their household maintains. Each family keeps its stretch of channel — dredged, staked, and read daily — and the water's condition is the family's public face."
  kind :culture
  subkind :cultural_identity
  status :complete
  topics :"outer-system", :household, :"social-structure", :ecology
  prevalence :uncommon
  appears_when all: { place: [:waterway] }

  descriptive_identity(
    manner: "Reedwater speech runs on water states — a person is 'running clear' or 'silting " \
            "up,' a plan 'finds its channel' — and family introductions name the stretch " \
            "before the surname, since the stretch is the surname's collateral.",
    attire: "Waxed channel dress and pole-shoes for the soft margins, with the family's " \
            "stake-mark worn as a clasp; crossing veterans add a plain cord at the wrist, " \
            "one knot per solo crossing witnessed.",
    hospitality: "A guest is poled across to the family island before anything else — " \
                 "arrival is a crossing, always — and the household's pride is that the " \
                 "guest's boots stay dry on family water."
  )

  prose <<~PROSE
    Dovra's channels shift with every flood season, and the Reedwater families are the reason the water stays legible. Each family holds a stretch — dredged, staked, its soft margins planted in the reeds that give the culture its name — and maintains it as tenure: the family that keeps a channel readable and crossable holds it, by a custom Korvath's courts long ago found wiser to record than to replace. The stretch is the family's public face. A clear-running, well-staked water announces its household the way a shopfront announces a trade, and 'their water tells you everything' is Reedwater's complete social verdict, deliverable in either direction.

    Adulthood is a crossing. A Reedwater child becomes an adult by taking a boat alone through the family stretch in flood season — reading the staked line, the silt, the day's water — with the family watching from the banks and forbidden by custom to call out. The crossing is witnessed, knotted onto the wrist cord, and final: one crossing, one verdict, and the rare refusals and failures are handled with a gentleness that surprises outsiders, since the culture's whole point is that the water gives the examination and the water is fair. Crossings draw kin from other stretches, the banks fill, and the moment the bow touches the family island the watching silence is allowed to become the loudest party the channel hosts all year.
  PROSE

  cue "The family island's banks are lined with silent watchers while one small boat works the staked line alone through flood water, and the silence has the texture of held breath."
  cue "Every adult wrist along the channel carries a plain knotted cord, and the knots are read as casually as faces."
  affordance "The Reedwater stretches are Dovra's living navigation system — staked, dredged, and read daily by families whose standing depends on the water's legibility — and a traveler on family water is safer than any chart could make them."
  pressure "Tenure follows maintenance, so a family in a bad year is watching its standing silt up in public — and the custom that neighbors may quietly dredge a struggling stretch is either the culture's kindest institution or its sharpest, depending on who tells it."
  variation "Delta families hold broad slow water and cross under sail; the upstream stretches are narrow and fast, and their crossings are the ones other Reedwater folk travel to watch."
  variation "The wrist cords accumulate witnessed crossings beyond the first — floods forded, rescues rowed — and an old poler's cord is read knot by knot at their funeral, aloud, as the eulogy's spine."
end
